Faster simulation: an optimised library build, and quicker eFuse reads

build_libqemu-esp32.sh configured the GPIO and I2C library with
--enable-debug, which builds QEMU at -O0 with debug-only checks. Without
it (debug info kept) the library is optimised like the executables: an
Arduino sketch boots on the ESP32 in 0.52 s instead of 0.87 s, and a busy
loop of GPIO reads runs several times faster. That alone stopped the DHT
library's interrupts-off read loop from tripping the interrupt watchdog
on a loaded host (20 sessions at once, none crashed).

hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c: an eFuse read or program completed 100 ms of
virtual time after it started (its comment said 10 ms). Real reads take
microseconds. Under -icount, where virtual time advances only as
instructions run, the ROM's poll of EFUSE_CMD took seconds of wall time
to cover 100 ms and boot looked hung. Now 1 ms; at 10 us the first boot
reported a software reset instead of power-on.

Written for esp32bench (GPL-2.0-or-later). Changes lcgamboa's
build_libqemu-esp32.sh and Espressif's hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c (Copyright
(c) 2019 Espressif Systems (Shanghai) Co. Ltd., GPL-2.0-or-later, notice
kept). Applied by esp32bench
(core/crates/sim/src/lcgamboa.rs) to lcgamboa/qemu at
885a6e7061fb70fac78a074a719423810bc19a17, after patch 0008.

diff --git a/build_libqemu-esp32.sh b/build_libqemu-esp32.sh
index 969e6bb..c5d0374 100755
--- a/build_libqemu-esp32.sh
+++ b/build_libqemu-esp32.sh
@@ -17,7 +17,7 @@ flags="$(./configure --help | perl -ne 'print if s/^  ([a-z][\w-]*) .*/\1/' | ta
 
 ${2:-.}/configure --target-list=$target --extra-cflags=-fPIC --disable-slirp $flags --enable-tcg \
 	--enable-system --disable-werror --disable-alsa  \
-        --enable-debug --enable-debug-info \
+        --enable-debug-info \
         --enable-gcrypt --enable-slirp --enable-pixman 
 	#--enable-gtk
 
diff --git a/hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c b/hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c
index 2a1241f..2bebd7d 100644
--- a/hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c
+++ b/hw/nvram/esp32_efuse.c
@@ -223,7 +223,12 @@ static void esp32_efuse_update_irq(Esp32EfuseState *s)
 static void esp32_efuse_op_timer_start(Esp32EfuseState *s)
 {
     uint64_t ns_now = qemu_clock_get_ns(QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL);
-    uint64_t interval_ns = 100000000; /* 10 ms, make this depend on EFUSE_CLK register */
+    /* esp32bench: 1 ms. It was 100 ms (its comment said 10 ms). With -icount, where
+     * virtual time moves only as instructions run, the ROM's poll of EFUSE_CMD (an
+     * MMIO access each time round) took seconds of wall time to cover 100 ms. Much
+     * shorter (10 us) and the first boot reported a software reset instead of
+     * power-on. */
+    uint64_t interval_ns = 1000000;
     timer_mod_anticipate_ns(&s->op_timer, ns_now + interval_ns);
 }
 
